This uses a Fenwick tree that stores the best subsequence length seen for each value range. Since the numbers can be negative or large, their sorted unique values are first mapped to positions from `1` onward.

For a current value, the tree gives the best length for values strictly smaller than it. Adding one gives the length of a subsequence ending at the current number, then the tree is updated at its compressed position.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int LIS(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> values = nums;
        sort(values.begin(), values.end());
        values.erase(unique(values.begin(), values.end()), values.end());

        vector<int> tree(values.size() + 1, 0);
        int answer = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            int position = lower_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), number) - values.begin() + 1;
            int bestBefore = query(tree, position - 1);
            int currentLength = bestBefore + 1;

            update(tree, position, currentLength);
            answer = max(answer, currentLength);
        }

        return answer;
    }

private:
    int query(const vector<int>& tree, int index) {
        int result = 0;
        while (index > 0) {
            result = max(result, tree[index]);
            index -= index & -index;
        }
        return result;
    }

    void update(vector<int>& tree, int index, int value) {
        while (index < static_cast<int>(tree.size())) {
            tree[index] = max(tree[index], value);
            index += index & -index;
        }
    }
};
```

The strict comparison is handled by querying `position - 1`, so equal numbers cannot extend one another. For example, `[2, 2, 2]` produces `1`, not `3`.

The input vector is only read. The sorted copy is extra memory, but coordinate compression allows this method to work with any integer range.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

The list called `tails` keeps one useful value for each possible subsequence length. When a number cannot extend the list, it replaces the first tail that is at least as large. That replacement keeps the same length but gives future numbers a smaller value to build on.

This code only returns the length. The values in `tails` should not be treated as the actual subsequence, because replacements can mix values from different subsequences.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int LIS(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> tails;

        for (int number : nums) {
            auto position = lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), number);

            if (position == tails.end()) {
                tails.push_back(number);
            } else {
                *position = number;
            }
        }

        return static_cast<int>(tails.size());
    }
};
```

One quick dry run with `[1, 6, 2, 3]` gives tails `[1]`, `[1, 6]`, `[1, 2]`, and `[1, 2, 3]`. The answer is `3`.

An empty input leaves `tails` empty and returns `0`. Duplicate values replace an existing tail because the search uses `lower_bound`, which is needed for a strictly increasing subsequence.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`. The input vector is unchanged.

This is the direct dynamic programming version. `dp[i]` stores the longest increasing subsequence that ends exactly at index `i`. Every element can start a subsequence of length one, then earlier smaller values can extend it.

The answer may finish anywhere, so the maximum value in `dp` is returned instead of only checking the last position.

```java
class Solution {
    public int LIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] dp = new int[n];
        int answer = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;

            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }

            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i]);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For `[4, 1, 2, 3]`, the values of `dp` become `[1, 1, 2, 3]`, so the result is `3` from `[1, 2, 3]`.

This is easy to check, but it compares many pairs. An empty array returns `0`, and equal values are not joined because the comparison is strictly less than.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

I kept a sorted `tails` list. Its values are not always one real subsequence, but `tails[i]` is the smallest ending value found for a subsequence of length `i + 1`. A smaller ending value gives later numbers more room to extend it.

For each number, binary search finds the first value that is greater than or equal to it. Replace that value, or append when the number is larger than every current tail.

```python
class Solution:
    def LIS(self, nums):
        tails = []

        for value in nums:
            left = 0
            right = len(tails)

            while left < right:
                middle = (left + right) // 2
                if tails[middle] < value:
                    left = middle + 1
                else:
                    right = middle

            if left == len(tails):
                tails.append(value)
            else:
                tails[left] = value

        return len(tails)
```

For example, `[10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7]` changes the tail list through `[10]`, `[9]`, `[2]`, `[2, 5]`, `[2, 3]`, and `[2, 3, 7]`. The final length is `3`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`. The input list is not changed.

At each position there are two choices: skip the number, or take it when it is greater than the last selected number. This version keeps the recursion visible, without caching repeated states, so it is useful as a simple starting point.

The `previousIndex` value of `-1` means that no number has been selected yet. A single-element array returns one, while an empty array reaches the base case and returns zero.

```java
class Solution {
    public int LIS(int[] nums) {
        return solve(nums, 0, -1);
    }

    private int solve(int[] nums, int index, int previousIndex) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            return 0;
        }

        int skip = solve(nums, index + 1, previousIndex);
        int take = 0;

        if (previousIndex == -1 || nums[index] > nums[previousIndex]) {
            take = 1 + solve(nums, index + 1, index);
        }

        return Math.max(skip, take);
    }
}
```

A small dry run for `[3, 1, 2]`: the recursion can skip `3`, take `1`, and then take `2`, giving length `2`. It also checks the branch that starts with `3`, even though that branch cannot be longer.

The code is short, but the same states are solved many times. For larger input, memoization or an iterative method is much safer.

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

