Instead of trying to build every possible subsequence, ask: “For a subsequence of each length, what is the smallest ending value that can be achieved?”

A smaller ending value is always better because it gives more future numbers a chance to extend the subsequence.

So we maintain the best tail for every length, and whenever a new number improves a tail, we replace it.
The number of lengths we can maintain = LIS length.

for example:

nums = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]

For each number x:

Find the first position where tails[pos] >= x
Replace it with x
If no such position exists, append x

10 → [10]

9 → [9]  (for length 1 tail , 9 is better alternative for inceasing subsequence than 10)

2 → [2]     (2 is better than 9, leaves more gap for increasing subsequence)

5 → [2,5]   ( 5 is greater than 2 , so append 5 , since its a inc subsequence)

3 → [2,3]   (3 replaced 5 here, because this is best possible tail at length 2 , it leaves more gap for an increasing subsequence as 3 < 5 )

7 → [2,3,7]     (7 is greater than last element , so append 7 )

101 → [2,3,7,101]     (101 is greater so append it )

18 → [2,3,7,18]    (18 replaces 101 again for same reason of giving better chance of increasing subsequence)

At each iteration we are not storing the final subsequence, but we are just book keeping the best possible tail element at each index of our sequence till that num[i]

answer = tails.size() = 4

As this will be longest tail size with increasing subsequence

Approach
tails[i] stores the smallest possible ending value of an increasing subsequence of length i+1.
For each x, find the first tails[i] >= x and replace it with x — a smaller tail gives more room to extend later.
If no such position exists, x extends the longest subsequence, so append it.
Therefore, tails.size() gives the length of the LIS.

